WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.942
A control is a process, activity, configuration, review, or safeguard that helps an organization meet a trust objective.

2
00:00:07.942 --> 00:00:14.248
Controls may be technical, administrative, operational, or a combination of several types.

3
00:00:14.248 --> 00:00:29.364
Examples include access reviews, change approvals, vulnerability management, incident response procedures, security awareness training, monitoring, backup testing, vendor review, risk assessment, and policy acknowledgment.

4
00:00:29.364 --> 00:00:34.253
Evidence is the record that shows a control exists and operates as intended.

5
00:00:34.253 --> 00:00:39.821
Auditors evaluate evidence to understand whether the organization followed the process it described.

6
00:00:39.821 --> 00:00:56.194
Evidence may include policies, procedures, tickets, approvals, screenshots, logs, reports, training records, meeting notes, system configurations, access review results, monitoring alerts, or incident records.

7
00:00:56.194 --> 00:00:58.828
Good evidence usually comes from normal work.

8
00:00:58.828 --> 00:01:10.791
If a team already uses tickets, code reviews, approval workflows, identity systems, monitoring platforms, and training tools consistently, those records can become audit evidence.

9
00:01:10.791 --> 00:01:13.076
Poor evidence practices create friction.

10
00:01:13.076 --> 00:01:22.894
If work happens informally, if approvals are not recorded, if tickets are missing context, or if ownership is unclear, teams may struggle to show what happened later.

11
00:01:22.894 --> 00:01:28.252
For employees, the lesson is simple: follow the process and document the work.

12
00:01:28.252 --> 00:01:34.758
Clear records help the organization demonstrate accountability, consistency, and operational discipline.

13
00:01:34.758 --> 00:01:39.827
Evidence quality improves when teams capture the right information at the time the work happens.

14
00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:45.764
Waiting until later often leads to missing context, unclear approvals, or unnecessary rework.
